From 6d8c80bf8907b0b9bc690197cdd02a5cd8f28abf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 14:45:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09264/11362] Give both arms the live agent's 3 tries, not
 exp5726's 1

Operator: "why did the test pass but the run fail? shouldn't we make changes to
ensure that the run is as successful as the test so that effort is not wasted?"

They were right and my stated reason for leaving it alone was wrong.

THE CONTRADICTION. The gate probe returned ok=True with both required symbols in
2,786s. The first real cell overran at 1,443s -- almost exactly one budget -- and
recorded induce_ok=False. Same model, same prompt, same n_ctx, same budget.

THE CAUSE, read from the source rather than guessed: `run_reason_cell_budget`
sets `prop.tries = 1` (exp5726 ~line 324) before calling the induce path. My gate
probe used `prop.tries`, which is 3. One attempt versus three.

WHY THAT OVERRIDE EXISTS, and why it does not belong here. exp5726 was studying
reasoning overrun; its docstring says "a /think overrun (reasoning past the
budget, no code) is recorded as induction_ok=False, the honest finding." For THAT
question, one attempt is the point. This experiment asks a different question --
which model induces better -- and inherited the constant with the harness.

WHAT SETTLED IT: the LIVE scored agent gets 3. `LocalGGUFProposer.tries` defaults
to 3 and every live induce call passes `tries=self.tries`. So tries=1 does not
describe the agent we ship, and measuring at 1 would have produced a number about
exp5726's experimental setup rather than about deployment.

I had argued against changing this on the grounds that raising tries "favours
Qwen because gemma does not need retries". That reasoning does not hold: the rule
is applied identically to both arms, gemma simply will not use the extra two. It
is the same shape as the n_ctx constant I already corrected -- a protocol value
that binds one model and not the other for reasons incidental to induction
quality. I corrected that one and then declined to correct this one, which was
inconsistent.

Both arms now get LIVE_TRIES = 3. Gemma's archived 38/39 was measured at tries=1;
it is being re-run here anyway, so both arms are measured under the same rule.

Cost goes back up -- a cell that needs its retries takes ~2,786s rather than
~1,443s -- but per the standing directive accuracy comes first, and a 15-hour run
to a foregone 0/39 is the waste actually worth avoiding.
